
/** \page "Building"

!!! Building with jam
\n
Jam is a make-like program that provides a versatile, unified build process 
across many platforms including automated unit-tests, cross-compilation and 
concurrent execution of build steps. This document intends to be a step-by-step
introduction to the jam-based build %system. Most examples demonstrate the usage
of jam on a windows NT build host, but differ only in the name of the jam
executable on other build hosts.

---
!! Requirements
It is usually not neccessary to install any additional tools since precompiled 
jam binaries for several platforms are provided in the 'jam' subdirectory of 
the project and nothing else is required. The following table lists the 
available precompiled binaries.

||OS||CPU||Jam binary
|Linux|x86|jam.linux.x86
|Linux|ppc|jam.linux.ppc
|Windows NT/XP|x86|jam.exe
|QNX6.3.0|x86|jam.qnx630.x86

If no precompiled tool is available for the desired platform, the complete 
source code can be found in the jam directory in the project tree. Makefiles 
for win32-based and posix-based systems are available. The jam binary itself
can be built with a regular C compiler and has no further dependencies than
the %system C runtime library.

---
!! Starting the build process
The jam tool must be invoked in the project root, where a 'jam' subdirectory 
and a file called 'Jambase' are located. If jam is run without any arguments, 
default build options are tried to be deduced from the environment. This will 
work well from a unix shell or the 'build command shell' provided by 
Microsoft visual studio installations. 

To start a build with default build options simply change to the project 
root directory and call the required jam binary. The command below builds
the project on NT:

@code
jam\jam.exe 
@endcode \n
Accordingly, on a power-pc linux %system the following command can be used
from the project root:

@code
./jam/jam.linux.ppc
@endcode \n
|#NOTE:#\n
After a checkout the jam binary needs to be made executable with
the chmod command. To do so for a linux/X86 binary enter the following
command:\n\n
#chmod +x ./jam/jam.linux.x86\n#

---
!! Building specific targets
All artefacts that are created during the build process are refered to as
'targets'. A target can be a binary file, such as a executable or a so-called
pseudo-target, which usually references other targets. All targets build
a dependency tree and if jam is invoked to build a target, all dependencies
will be resolved automatically and be built first.
To build a specific target of the project simply invoke jam with 
a target name. The following command will build the target program.exe and 
all its dependencies:

@code
jam\jam.exe program.exe
@endcode \n
Some important pseudo-targets are all, lib or exe. The target 'all' will 
build  all targets in the dependency tree. 'lib' will build only libraries
and 'exe' only exectuables, respectively. When jam is called without 
specifying any targets the pseudo-target 'all' is assumed. This is the same 
as explicitly calling jam to build the target 'all'. To build all libraries
and their dependencies in a project, invoke jam like so:

@code
jam\jam.exe lib
@endcode

---
!! Rebuilding targets
To completely rebuild a target and its dependencies, the command line 
parameter -a can be passed to jam. The option -a can be used with 
or without a specific target. Thus the following command will rebuild 
the whole project:

@code
jam\jam.exe -a
@endcode \n
A specific target can be rebuild by combining -a and a target name. The target
program.exe will be completely rebuild with the following command:

@code
jam\jam.exe -a program.exe
@endcode

---
!! Cleaning the project
The pseudo-target 'clean' plays a special role in the jam build %system. All
artefacts that are created during the build process are dependents of
the 'clean' peseudo-target. When jam is invoked with the target clean all 
artefacts will be deleted if they exist. Therefore the following command
will remove all artefacts that were created by a build with default options:

@code
jam\jam.exe clean
@endcode

---
!! Command line options
Several command line arguments can be passed to jam:

||Argument||Description
|-a|Build target(s), even if they are current.
|-dx|Set the debug level to x
|-jx|Run up to x shell command concurrently
|-n|Don't actually execute, but show the updating actions.
|-q|Quit quickly as soon as a target fails.
|-sx=y|Set variable x=y, overriding environment.

---
!! Build options
Various build options can be set to control how jam builds the targets, 
for example if a release or debug version should be built. Build
options are set by special jam variables before or when jam is
executed. 
A jam variable can be set with the command line option -s followed by a 
variable/value pair separated by the equal sign (-sVARIABLE=VALUE) or by
setting a environment variable with the same name. When jam is invoked, all 
environment variables will be available as jam variables in the build scripts.

One important jam variable is named CONFIG and can be set to 'debug' or 
'release' to build either a debug or release version of a project. To 
build a release version of a project, use the following command:

@code
jam\jam.exe -sCONFIG=release
@endcode \n
Most variables have default values or are deduced from environment variables.
The default for CONFIG is 'debug'. The following table gives an overview
which jam variables are used to enable certain build options:

||Argument||Description||Possible Values||Default %Value
|TARGET_OS|Target operating %system|nt / linux / qnx-nto / wince| same as host OS
|TARGET_OSPLAT|Target Platform|x86 / arm /mips| same as host CPU
|CONFIG|Optimisation|debug / release|debug
|TOOLSET|Compiler/build tools to be used| vc7 / vc8 / gcc /qcc / evc4| deduced from environment
|BYTEORDER|Endianess of the target CPU| le / be|processor dependent

More than one variable can be set when jam is invoked. The following command 
builds a release version with the gnu compiler toolset on a linux host:

@code
./jam/jam.linux.x86 -sCONFIG=release -sTOOLSET=gcc
@endcode


!! Compiling for Linux with GCC

Compiling for Linux with the GNU compiler is done from a normal shell. The 
default value for the TOOLSET is gcc. The following command builds a release 
version on a X86 linux %system:

@code
./jam/jam.linux.x86 -sCONFIG=release
@endcode

---
!! Compiling for Windows NT/XP with Visual Studio 7/8

The Microsoft visual studio installation contains a special 'build command 
shell'. It differs from a regular command shell in that more environment 
variables are set. If the build command shell is not used, the VISUALC variable 
has to be set either as environment variable or with -s when jam is invoked. 
If the path to the visual studio installation contains whitespace the path has 
to be put in quotes. The following command demonstrates how a build can be 
started from a regular console on windows to build with VisualC++ 2003 (vc7):

@code
jam\jam.exe -sVISUALC="C:\Programme\Microsoft Visual Studio 2003 .Net" 
            -sTOOLSET=vc7
@endcode \n
If the special 'build command shell' is used, VISUALC is determined from other 
environment variables and does not have to be specified. Also, the TOOLSET is 
determined from the environment and does not have to be specified. It is not 
possible to build vc7 targets from the 'build command shell' provided by a 
Microsoft visual studio 8 installation. 

---
!! Compiling Windows CE targets

Windows CE targets need to be cross compiled under Windows NT/XP. Therefore 
the TARGET_OS and TARGET_OSPLAT variables need to be set. No build command
shell for embedded targets is provided by Microsoft, so the path to the 
Visual studio installation has to be set. The following command builds for 
ARM CPU's:

@code
./jam/jam.exe -sVISUALC="C:\Programme\Microsoft Visual Studio 2003 .Net" 
              -sTOOLSET=vc8 -sTARGET_OS=wince -sTARGET_OSPLAT=arm
@endcode

---
!! Compiling QNX targets

%Building with jam on a QNX host is not much different than on unix systems.
To build on a X86 QNX %system enter:

@code
./jam/jam.qnx630.x86
@endcode \n
QNX targets can be cross compiled or directly compiled on a QNX host. Cross 
compiling can be done on either a QNX host for another CPU or on a 
Windows NT/XP host. For cross compiling the TARGET_OS and TARGET_OSPLAT 
variables need to be set. The following command will build a project on a 
Windows NT/XP host for QNX systems with X86 processors.

@code
./jam/jam.exe -sTARGET_OS=qnx630 -sTARGET_OSPLAT=x86
@endcode \n
To cross compile for QNX on a QNX host the TARGET_OSPLAT needs to be set:

@code
./jam/jam.qnx630.x86 -sTARGET_OSPLAT=ppc
@endcode

===
!! Setting up a new Project
Jam requires a few files and one directory to be present to be able to compile
a project.

- #'jam' subdirectory#\n
The 'jam' subdirectory must be located in the project root. It contains 
precompiled jam binaries for the most common platforms and a libraray of
Jamrules for different platforms, toolkits and configurations. This
library is usually called the Jambase.

- #'Jambase' file#\n
The Jambase file must be located in the project root and contains the %system
to 'boot-strap' a jam build. When jam is invoked, it looks for a file called
Jambase to import its build library.

- #'Jamrules' file#\n
The file Jamrules is optional and is located in the project root. It contains
build rules that extend the Jambase for project specific rules and variables.

- #'Jamfile' file#\n
A 'Jamfile' file must be located in the project root. It usually subincludes
further Jamfiles in subdirectories of the project, so that in a typical
jam project all subdirectories contain a Jamfile. The Jamfile sepcifies what
targets should be built.


|#Rules and Variable Syntax#\n
A Jam variable is global and can be assigned with the equal sign. To get
the value in a variable the \$-sign has to be used:
\pre VAR = 5 ;
 Echo "The value is:" $(VAR) ;
\endpre
The so called Jambase in the jam subdirectory and the rules taken from the
file Jamrules can be used in the Jamfiles of the project to build targets.
Rules can take multiple parameters which are separated by a colon. For Example
the rule %File, which copies files, can takes two parameters, the first is the 
target file, the second the source file:
\pre File target.txt : source.txt ;
\endpre
}Note that the jam syntax requires all tokens to be whitespace-separated including
the semicolon at the end of each line.}

---
!! Handling Directory Trees
Jam defines a special variable called TOP. This variable contains the path
to the project root directory. Path names can be constructed rooted with
TOP. The SubDir and SubInclude rules used to build the project tree take
paths rooted with TOP.

The SubDir rule must be the first rule invoked in a Jamfile. It specifies
the path to the current directory and initializes a set of variables that are
used by other rules to uniquely identify the source files and assign locations.
The SubDir rule takes as its first argument the root variable's name and takes 
as subsequent parameters the directory names leading from the root to the 
directory of the current Jamfile. The following means that the Jamfile which
contains the SubDir statement is in the the directory $(TOP)/src/util: 

@code
SubDir $(TOP) src util
@endcode \n
The Jamfile in the project root directory therefore simply sets its directory
to $(TOP) with the SubDir rule.

The SubInclude rule is used  in a Jamfile to include another Jamfile and to
build up the project directory tree this way. Its arguments are in the same 
format as SubDir's. The recommended practice is to include only one level 
of subdirectories at a time and let the Jamfile in each subdirectory include 
its own subdirectories. The following Jamfile includes four other Jamfiles:

@code
SubInclude $(TOP) src
SubInclude $(TOP) man
SubInclude $(TOP) util
SubInclude $(TOP) misc
@endcode \n
The SubInclude statements must be the last statements in a Jamfile, because it
is affected by the SubDir rule of the included Jamfiles. All paths are relative
to the root directory.

---
!! Setting Include Paths
The rule 'SubDirHdrs' sets include paths for all source files in a directory. 
The following jam statement will add the path $(PPR_TOP)/include to the header
search paths:

@code
SubDirHdrs $(TOP)/include ;
@endcode

---
!! Building Executables
The rule 'Main' can be used to build executables from a list of source files. 
The following example assumes that besides the mandatory jam files a file 
called main.cpp resides in the project tree that contains program code. To 
build a program called 'program', the Main rule must be applied in the Jamfile:

@code
Main program : main.cpp ;
@endcode \n
The first parameter passed to the rule is the target name, the second is 
the list of source files. All %system specific target name decorations will 
be added by jam auomatically. So under windows the resulting binary will 
be called program.exe. Under unix it will simply be called 'program'.

---
!! Building static libraries
The rule 'Library' can be used to build a static library from a list of source 
files. The following example assumes that besides the mandatory jam file a file
called example.cpp resides in the project tree that contains the library code.
To build a static library called 'example' the Library rule must be used in the
corresponding Jamfile:

@code
Library example : example.cpp ;
@endcode \n
The first parameter passed to the rule is the target name, the second is 
the list of source files. All %system specific target name decorations will 
be added by jam auomatically. So under windows the resulting library will 
be called example.lib. Under unix it will be called libexample.a.

---
!! Building shared libraries
The rule 'SharedLibrary' can be used to build a shared library from a list of 
source files. The following example assumes that besides the mandatory jam 
files, a file called example.cpp resides in the project tree that contains 
the library code. To build a shared library called 'example' the SharedLibrary 
rule must be used in the corresponding Jamfile:

@code
SharedLibrary example : example.cpp ;
@endcode \n
The first parameter passed to the rule is the target name, the second is 
the list of source files. All %system specific target name decorations will 
be added by jam auomatically. So under windows the resulting library will 
be called example.dll and a import library called example.lib will be created. 
Under unix, one shared library called libexample.so will be created.

---
!! Building Modules
Building a module means simply to build a shared and a static library at the 
same time. The rule 'Module' can be used to build a module from a list of 
source files. The following example assumes that besides the mandatory jam 
files, a file called 'module.cpp' resides in the project tree that contains 
the module code. The Module rule can be used in the corresponding Jamfile 
like this:

@code
Module module : module.cpp ;
@endcode \n
The first parameter passed to the rule is the module target name, the second is 
the list of source files. 
All %system specific target name decorations will 
be added by jam auomatically. The resulting static library will be decorated 
with the suffix '-static'. Thus, under windows the resulting static library will 
be called module-static.lib and under unix the static library will be called 
libmodule-static.a. 
The resulting shared library will be called module.dll, with an import library 
called module.lib under windows. For unix only a shared library named 
libmodule.so will be built.

---
!!Linking Executables
Separate rules exist to link shared or static libraries to an executable. The 
rule Main_LinkLibraries can be used to link a number of static libraries to
an executable. This example assumes that a library called example is build from
a source file example.cpp and linked to a program called 'program', which
is to be built from a source file called main.cpp. The Jamfile in this case
looks like this:

@code
Library example : example.cpp ;

Main program : main.cpp ;
Main_LinkLibraries program : example ;
@endcode \n
The first parameter passed to the rule 'Main_LinkLibraries' is the name 
of the target the static libraries should be linked to, the second is 
the list of static libraries to link.

The rule Main_LinkSharedLibraries can be used to link a number of shared 
libraries to an executable. This example assumes that a library called 
example is build from a source file example.cpp and linked to a program 
called 'program', which is to be built from a source file called main.cpp.
The Jamfile in this case looks like this:

@code
SharedLibrary example : example.cpp ;

Main program : main.cpp ;
Main_LinkLibraries program : example ;
@endcode \n
The first parameter passed to the rule 'Main_LinkSharedLibraries' is the 
name of the target the shared libraries should be linked to, the second 
is the list of shared libraries to link.

---
!!Linking Shared Libraries
Separate rules exist to link shared or static libraries to a shared library. 
The rule SharedLibrary_LinkLibraries can be used to link a number of static 
libraries to a shared library. This example assumes that a library called 
tire is build from a source file tire.cpp and linked to a shared library 
called 'car', which is to be built from a source file called car.cpp.
The Jamfile in this case looks like this:

@code
Library tire : tire.cpp ;

SharedLibrary car : car.cpp ;
SharedLibrary_LinkLibraries car : tire ;
@endcode \n
The first parameter passed to the rule 'SharedLibrary_LinkLibraries' is 
the name of the shared library target the static libraries should be linked 
to, the second is the list of static libraries to link.

The rule SharedLibrary_LinkSharedLibraries can be used to link a number 
of shared libraries to another shared library. This example assumes that 
a shared library called tire is build from a source file tire.cpp and 
linked to a shared library called 'car', which is to be built from a source 
file called car.cpp. The Jamfile in this case looks like this:

@code
SharedLibrary tire : tire.cpp ;

SharedLibrary car : car.cpp ;
SharedLibrary_LinkSharedLibraries car : tire ;
@endcode \n
The first parameter passed to the rule 'SharedLibrary_LinkSharedLibraries' 
is the name of the target the shared libraries should be linked to, the 
second is the list of shared libraries to link.


*/